# std/MIGRATION — Moving to the v1.0.0 Layout

File: std/docs/MIGRATION.oot

This file describes how to migrate code from the old monorepo
layout to the std/ v1.0.0 layout. The 1.0.0 cut is a hard cut.
There is no compat layer. There are no shims. Old paths are dead.

## 1. The old layout

Before 1.0.0, the standard library lived inside the compiler
monorepo. The compiler was at the top of the tree, and the standard
library sat next to it under `std/`. There was no separate package.
There was no `api_surface` check. Some modules lived at the top
level (`std/ai/`, `std/robot/`, `std/bio/`, `std/chem/`, ...) and
some lived nested (`std/phys/`, `std/sec/`, ...).

## 2. The new layout

At 1.0.0, the standard library is a separate repo:
`openOODA/std`. The compiler stays in its own repo. The package
manager (`opm`) installs std/ as a dependency. The 8 domains
(`app/`, `core/`, `fs/`, `hw/`, `meta/`, `net/`, `science/`,
`sec/`) sit at the top of the repo. The `compile/` directory was
removed; its contents now live in the compiler repo, not in std/.

## 3. The path changes that matter

The reorg took 7 phases. Each phase is named inside the ANCHOR.oo
of the affected domain. The headline moves are:

  - std/cli/                  -> std/core/cli/
  - std/collection/           -> std/core/collection/
  - std/encoding/             -> std/core/encoding/
  - std/format/ and std/fmt/  -> std/core/format/
  - std/io/                   -> std/core/io/
  - std/markup/ and std/json/ -> std/core/markup/
  - std/math/                 -> std/core/math/
  - std/core/                 -> std/core/primitives/
  - std/principles/           -> std/core/principles/
  - std/proofs/               -> std/core/proofs/
  - std/text/                 -> std/core/text/

  - std/test/                 -> std/meta/test/
  - std/diag/                 -> std/meta/diag/

  - std/ai/                   -> std/app/ai/
  - std/robot/                -> std/app/robot/
  - std/ui/                   -> std/app/ui/
  - std/data/                 -> std/app/data/
  - std/ai/{synthesizer, repair, evolution, pair_programmer, repl_engine}.oo
                              -> std/app/agent/
  - std/phys/weapons/ + std/phys/aero/
                              -> std/app/combat/

  - std/bio/                  -> std/science/bio/
  - std/chem/                 -> std/science/chem/
  - std/phys/satellite_dvb/   -> std/science/comm/
  - std/econ/                 -> std/science/econ/
  - std/phys/                 -> std/science/phys/

  - net/wire/ + net/web_protocols/ + net/http3/ + net/advanced/
                              -> net/protocol/
  - net/telecom/ + net/telecom_5g/ + net/telecom_6g/
                              -> net/transport/
  - net/p2p_mesh/ + net/edge/ -> net/mesh/
  - net/{packet_capture, ids, dpi}  -> net/telemetry/
  - std/sync/                 -> std/net/sync/
  - std/actor/                -> std/net/actor/

  - std/wire/                 -> std/core/encoding/wire/
  - ml_lib/                   -> std/app/ai/  (merged into ml/)

## 4. How to update your imports

Three steps:

  1. Find every `import "std/..."` in your code.
  2. Rewrite the path to the new location. Use the table above.
  3. Run the loader. It will fail closed if any path is wrong or
     if a private sibling is imported by mistake.

The recommended pattern: import the top-level ANCHOR.oo for the
domain you need, not a deep file. Example:

  import "std/core/ANCHOR.oo";
  import "std/net/ANCHOR.oo";

This pulls in the full public surface and keeps the api_surface=8
contract intact. Do not import `_law`, `_theory`, `_principle`,
`_invariants`, `_kat`, or `_stubs` files. Those are private and the
loader will reject them from outside the repo.

## 5. The api_surface=8 contract

The VERSION file in this repo declares `api_surface=8`. This is a
structural check, not a behavioral one. At load time the loader
reads std/ANCHOR.oo and counts the public sub-directories that it
re-exports. If the count is not 8, the loader fails closed. This
guarantees that no future patch can quietly add a 9th domain or
remove one of the 8. The contract is binary: 8 or fail.

Consumers of std/ do not need to re-implement this check. The
loader does it for them. But consumers should still pin the std
version in their own lockfile. See INSTALL.oot for the version
compatibility contract.

## 6. What to do if a module is missing

If a path you used to import no longer exists, the table in
section 3 is the first place to look. If the module is still
listed there as having moved, follow the move. If it is not in the
table, the module was removed. The removal is intentional. Check
the audit/ directory and the qa/ directory for the replacement or
for the formal proof that the removed surface was redundant.
